{
  "requirement": "`trust_but_verify::agent::Tool` supplies the name and description shown to the\nmodel. `execute` can await work using the context of the current criterion.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Tool<D: Domain, A, R>: Send + Sync {\n    fn name(&self) -> &str;\n    fn description(&self) -> &str;\n    fn execute(&self, cx: &ValidationContext<'_, D>, args: A,)\n        -> impl Future<Output = R> + Send;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has two obligations. The tool supplies the name and description shown to the model. `execute` can await work using the current criterion's context. `registered_tools_use_the_current_context_and_ordinary_node_ids` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs` covers both.\n\n**Name and description shown to the model**\n- The test registers `Lookup(\"lookup_requirements\")` and `Inspect` through `with_tool`.\n- It asserts the exact tool names in the first four requests. They are `read_node`, `outgoing_edges`, `get_next_page`, `submit_result`, `lookup_requirements`, `inspect`.\n- It asserts that `lookup_requirements` carries the description \"Find requirement node IDs matching a name glob.\", which `Lookup::description` supplies.\n- The name comes from `Lookup(&'static str)`, which is a different tool name. The second domain registers `Lookup(\"other\")`. The test asserts that `other` appears in the request tools and that `inspect` and `lookup_requirements` do not.\n- `Inspect::description` (\"Read an entry.\") is never asserted. This is not a gap, because the trait method `description` is the same one for every tool and the `Lookup` assertion exercises it.\n\n**`execute` uses the current criterion's context**\n- `Lookup::execute` uses `cx.graph()` to record the pattern. It also interns node IDs through the context's graph.\n- The test asserts `lookup_calls == [\"requirements.sdk.*\"]`. It asserts that the first tool output is `[1, 2]`.\n- `Inspect::execute` calls `cx.read` and `cx.outgoing_edges`. The test asserts that `cx.dependencies()` then holds exactly the `Node` and `OutgoingEdges(..., \"related\")` dependencies. This shows the tool ran against the current context.\n- The test asserts that the `inspect` output equals the text of the outline definition.\n- Both tools are async fns driven by the agent loop, so the awaited future form is exercised. The test also covers the invalid-argument case and the usage statistics, which do not bear on this requirement beyond context.\n\nI found no requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept.",
